## Common Problems and Solutions

When troubleshooting, you will usually need the FOSSBilling log files. Their location is covered in [Support](/support/faq/#where-can-i-find-error-logs).

## HTTP Error 404 / Can only access client dashboard

### Subfolder installations

Install FOSSBilling in the document root of its own domain or subdomain, such as `https://billing.example.com`. Subfolder URLs such as `https://example.com/billing` are unsupported.

### Apache, LiteSpeed, or OpenLiteSpeed

If you are using Apache, LiteSpeed, or OpenLiteSpeed and receive HTTP 404 errors in parts of FOSSBilling, check the following:

- Ensure that you have `mod_rewrite` installed and enabled.
- Verify there is a valid `.htaccess` file in the same directory as the FOSSBilling `index.php` file.
- If you do have a valid `.htaccess` file, verify that it matches the [default FOSSBilling one](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/main/src/.htaccess).
- If you are using OpenLiteSpeed, restart the service so the updated `.htaccess` file is loaded.
- If you have checked all of the above, restart the web server. Some configuration changes are not applied until the service reloads.

### Apache

- Ensure that `AllowOverride All` is enabled.
- See [this Stack Overflow answer](https://stackoverflow.com/a/22526144) for background if you are not sure where to configure it.

### NGINX

- Verify that you loaded the recommended [NGINX configuration](/getting-started/installation/#web-server-configuration).

## Permission issues

Start with the path reported by the installer, updater, or [application logs](/support/faq/#where-can-i-find-error-logs).

### File and folder permissions

- Check that PHP can read the application files and `config.php`. Restrict access to `config.php` to the accounts that need it.
- Check that PHP can write to the cache, log, and upload directories. These are normally under `data/`. If you changed `path_data`, check the configured location for the cache and logs.
- During installation, PHP needs to create `config.php` in the installation directory. For updates started from the admin panel, use the paths reported by the updater's readiness check to identify any additional write permissions needed.

### File ownership

Find the account that runs PHP for your site in your hosting control panel or server configuration. For PHP-FPM, check the site's pool `user` and `group` settings. Check the affected path's owner, group, and permissions, then correct access for that account. [PHP-FPM pool configuration](https://www.php.net/manual/en/install.fpm.configuration.php#fpm.configuration.user)

### RHEL Linux (and derivatives)

If Unix permissions look correct on a system running SELinux, check the audit log for a denial involving the web PHP process and the affected path. Keep SELinux enforcing and correct the labeling or policy for the specific denial. [Red Hat SELinux troubleshooting](https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/8/html/using_selinux/troubleshooting-problems-related-to-selinux_using-selinux)

## Unknown "encore_entry_link_tags" function.

If a custom theme still uses this Twig function, follow the [theme asset migration instructions](/maintenance/updating/0-7-to-0-8/#asset-loading) to replace it.

## Errors after updating

If FOSSBilling starts failing after an update, work through these steps:

1. Review the [updating guide](/maintenance/updating/) and [changelog](/maintenance/changelog/) for any breaking changes or manual actions required.
2. Check whether [update finalization](/maintenance/updating/#update-finalization) is still pending. Follow the command-line fallback there if you cannot access the admin panel.
3. If you're still experiencing issues, you can ask for further assistance via our [Discord](https://fossbilling.org/discord), or with a [bug report](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/issues).

## Clearing the Cache

In the admin sidebar, go to **System → Settings** and select the **System** module. Open the **Cache** tab and click **Invalidate cache** to clear compiled templates and cached application state.

## Issues with cron

If you see a warning like this in the FOSSBilling dashboard, cron has not run for at least 15 minutes. That is a real problem because many core tasks depend on it.
![FOSSBilling warning about cron execution](../../../assets/guides/install/cron-warning.png)

1. Check the FOSSBilling [logs](/support/faq/#where-can-i-find-error-logs) for any PHP or application errors.
2. Confirm that cron is configured to run every five minutes. FOSSBilling shows a crontab example with the correct path for your installation under the cron settings (`/admin/extension/settings/cron`).
3. If you're using a control panel with multiple users, log into your user account via the CLI and enter `php -v` to see what the default PHP version is under your account. If it's less than FOSSBilling's minimum PHP version, follow the below steps to correct it.

### An incorrect PHP version is being used

Because cron runs as a server command, it is not tied to the PHP version configured for a specific site. Control panels often default to an older system PHP binary, which can make the cron job fail if that version is below FOSSBilling's minimum requirement.

These steps may help you resolve this issue:

1. If your control panel provides a PHP selector for cron, choose a version that meets FOSSBilling's requirements.
2. Try specifying a different PHP binary in the cron command, for example `php8.3` or `/usr/bin/php8.3` instead of just `php`.
3. Remove unsupported PHP versions if they keep being selected by default.
4. Ask your hosting or control-panel provider how to set a different PHP version for cron jobs.
5. As a last resort, use the [cron API endpoint](/getting-started/installation/#configuring-the-cronjob) with `wget` or an external cron service.
   - Be aware that cron can run for a while. Triggering it over HTTP is more likely to hit timeout limits than running it locally.

## Administrator account login issues

- Verify that you are using the administrator login page. The client and admin areas are separate, and the default admin prefix is `/admin` (`https://example.com/admin`).
- [Manually reset your admin password](/maintenance/admin-manual-reset).

## `curl_multi_exec` errors

If you are seeing errors involving `curl_multi_exec`, your control panel or host has probably disabled that PHP function.
You will need to re-enable it before FOSSBilling can work correctly on that server.
